class Solution {
  public int roundDeskTotalReceipt(int[] readings, int k) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int[]remaining=a.clone(),done=new int[a.length];Deque<Integer>queue=new ArrayDeque<>();for(int i=0;i<a.length;i++)if(a[i]>0)queue.add(i);int time=0;while(!queue.isEmpty()){int i=queue.remove(),served=Math.min(k,remaining[i]);remaining[i]-=served;time+=served;if(remaining[i]>0)queue.add(i);else done[i]=time;}for(int x:done)out.add(x);}
    int answer=0;for(int x:out)answer+=x;return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build finishTimes

Each number in `readings` is the work time needed by a job. All jobs are ready at time 0, in input order. The worker gives the first job up to `k` time units. If it still needs work, put it at the back of the line. Then serve the next job. Record when each job finishes, measured from time 0. Record these finish times in original job order. A job needing 0 time finishes at time 0 and uses no worker time.

Return the answer

Add up every number in `finishTimes`. Store the sum in `total`. Complete `roundDeskTotalReceipt` so it returns `total`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `finishTimes` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n + total/k); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length; total is all job work time added together. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

